Find $C+D$ (in base 10), given the following addition problem in base 6:
\[
\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\;}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}
& & & 5 & C & D_{6}\\ 
&+& & & 5 & 2_{6}\\ 
\cline{2-6}
& & & 1 & 2 & C_{6}\\ 
\end{array} 
\]

s:
To solve $2033_4 \div 22_4$, we perform long division in base 4:
\[ 
\begin{array}{cc|cccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 1 & 1 \\
\cline{3-6}
2 & 2 & 2 & 0 & 3 & 3 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} && 2&2& \downarrow \\ \cline{4-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} && 0&2&3 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} && & 2&2 \\ \cline{5-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} && & & 1 \\
\end{array}
\]
In base 10, $22_4 = 2 \cdot 4 + 2 = 10$, and $2033_4 = 2 \cdot 64 + 0 \cdot 16 + 3 \cdot 4 + 3 = 131$. So, $131 \div 10 = 13.1$

In base 4, $13_{10} = 31_4$ (dividing 13 by 4 gives a quotient of 3 and a remainder of 1, so $13_{10} = 31_4$). The division has a remainder of 1.

Thus, the quotient of $2033_4 \div 22_4$ is $\boxed{11_4}$.

